<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Tinh Hai Circuit</h1><p class="st-meta">866 CE – 938 CE · capital Dai La</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The late Tang military circuit in northern Vietnam whose local governors, the Khuc family, laid the groundwork for Vietnamese independence.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-pian">Gao Pian</a> expelled <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nanzhao">Nanzhao</a> from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/protectorate-general-to-pacify-the-south">Protectorate General to Pacify the South</a> in 866, the region was reorganised as Jinghai, in Vietnamese Tinh Hai, a military circuit governed by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiedushi">jiedushi</a>. Its seat was the citadel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dai-la">Dai La</a>, on the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> disintegrated, local leaders took over. In 905 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuc-thua-du">Khuc Thua Du</a>, a powerful local landowner, seized the governorship, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuc-hao">Khuc Hao</a> reformed taxes and administration. The circuit was nominally loyal to Chinese courts while acting autonomously. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-han">Southern Han</a>, the new regime in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a>, tried to reassert control in 930.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duong-dinh-nghe">Duong Dinh Nghe</a> expelled the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-han">Southern Han</a> in 931 but was murdered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kieu-cong-tien">Kieu Cong Tien</a> in 937. Kieu called in the Southern Han, and in 938 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngo-quyen">Ngo Quyen</a> defeated their fleet at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bach-dang-river">Bach Dang</a> river, ending a millennium of Chinese rule and founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ngo-dynasty">Ngo dynasty</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tinh-hai-circuit.jpg" alt="Tinh Hai Circuit" width="320" height="228"><figcaption>Image: SS · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Later_Liang.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>866 CE – 938 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dai La</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Khuc Thua Du, Khuc Hao, Duong Dinh Nghe</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Chinese folk religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Vietnamese, Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jinghai Circuit, Tĩnh Hải quân, Khuc clan autonomy</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=902&amp;focus=tinh-hai-circuit" aria-label="Tinh Hai Circuit on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7522 1381 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tinh Hai Circuit lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7771 1192h0M7855 1179h0M8025 1580h0M7846 1653h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7842 1637h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7842 1637h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Vietnam. <a href="/atlas?year=902&amp;focus=tinh-hai-circuit">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
